{
  "brief_rationale": "The doctor met the core safety chain: they screened biliary red flags, reconstructed the discharge/follow-up state, repeatedly separated emergency triggers from outpatient hepatobiliary follow-up, and handled poor intake with insulin/acarbose safety boundaries. They also completed multiple high-order actions, especially reconstructing the transition state and dynamically reprioritizing diabetes medication safety. However, after the patient had undergone cholecystectomy and been discharged, the doctor briefly reverted to advice to ask whether/when surgery was needed, then corrected only after the family pointed it out; this is a minor/moderate in-chain non-overreach/continuity error under RC7. Under the frozen rubric, any minor responsibility-chain error caps the trajectory at grade 2 despite later mitigation and strong overall performance.",
